                0.901,
                0.313
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 13.10 (a): Global surface ocean currents showing circulation like gyres"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.346,
            0.565,
            0.601
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ocean currents play a major role in regulating the Earth’s climate and supporting life. By transporting heat from the equator towards the poles, they reduce temperature differences across the planet. For example, the warm ocean current — the North Atlantic Drift (Fig. 13.10b), an extension of the Gulf Stream (a strong ocean current carrying warm water from the southern part of the North American east coast across the Atlantic Ocean) — flows toward the northwestern coast of Europe and keeps many ports ice-free during winter, even at high latitudes. This moderating influence on climate also supports human activities, such as trade and commerce. Ocean currents also support a massive ecosystem by transporting nutrients."
            }
          ]
        },
